00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.100
and it gets me into the right mindset

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.240
So my next tip is to study for the specific technology.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.040
Now I'm leaving this at like a 1.5, because this may not apply to everyone.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.960
If you're interviewing for a role specializing in a specific technology or a stack—

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.190
And you'll know this by looking at the job description

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:25.660
then you're definitely going to want to study the concepts associated to that technology or stack

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.180
For instance, I'm an iOS engineer and so when I'm studying for interviews

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:34.100
I'm brushing up on core iOS concepts and frameworks like UI kit for example my

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.860
My friend and web engineer friends will brush up on things like JavaScript and react instead

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:44.369
Using this knowledge as part of your technical interview will exemplify your level of mastery with this technology

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:48.740
It will give companies a better sense of where you stand in terms of your experience

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.500
Okay, so my next tip is to study with a buddy

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.540
This is actually the most important piece of advice that I have for you in studying for a technical interview

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:02.240
We mentioned a little bit about this in my Q&amp;A with Jarvis video,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:08.060
but when companies are giving you a technical interview, They're not as interested in you having the right answer

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.720
as they are seeing what your thought process is like.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.960
"But Mayuko, didn't you just say you needed to brush up on core technical concepts

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.960
in order to do well in your technical interview?"

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.080
Well, yeah! But the way that I think about it is

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.260
that you're better equipped to solve these problems if you have the right tools in hand

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.620
Those tools in this case are being able to recall computer science concepts and frameworks

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.290
in order for you to solve your problem well!

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:34.240
Imagine if someone told you to build a table without any tools

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:38.120
you're much better off successfully figuring out how to build the table

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:43.220
if you have those tools right? So once you have your technical chops back up to snuff from studying

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.000
then find a buddy to do a mock interview with you they can pretend to be the

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.380
interviewer and give you the interview problem.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.200
you can practice thinking out loud as you would in a real technical interview and the act of doing this

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.080
will just get you so much more comfortable with articulating your thought process

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:04.040
Now, not articulating your thought process in a technical interview is actually where most interviews go awry

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.360
If you're not explaining what you're thinking

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.100
then your interviewer has no chance to course-correct you towards the right direction

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.620
And they actually don't get very much information or insight into how you solve problems

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.940
which is exactly what they want to find out in this interview!

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:23.300
So, try this out with a buddy and at the end of your mock interview, ask your feedback on what you did well

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:24.660
and what you didn't do well.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:29.620
you can take this feedback as ways to improve your next mock interview or for your actual technical interview

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.980
My last piece of feedback is to relax and you're gonna be just fine

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:39.780
I know that interviews are scary and talking to an interviewer to prove your worth is downright terrifying

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.420
But know that you are going to own this!

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.900
If you (1) study and (2) practice with a buddy, then know that you can walk in there with confidence

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:50.900
because you've prepared for your technical interview!

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.400
Do whatever it is that you need to do in order to get yourself amped and ready for this interview!

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.879
I personally like power posing for two minutes right before my interview

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.750
Also remember that everything is a process and so there might be some rockiness along the way

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.740
Interviews can be really competitive

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.040
There could only be one spot, but a thousand applicants

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:17.420
But know that preparing and going in there and giving it your all is so much better than not trying at all

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.420
Do or do not there is no try

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:21.900
With all things in life

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.960
remember that each bump in the road is a new lesson that can be learned that you can take with you next time

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.480
so regardless of the result, I highly recommend you follow up with your interviewer if you can

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.739
to ask for feedback on what you can improve on

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:39.049
they're usually more than willing to help on this and you can take these lessons with you for your next interview

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:44.580
Just remember to take a deep breath and stay positive during your interview.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.440
Be polite, inquisitive and thoughtful and you're going to do great!

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.720
I know that you can do this!

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.960
So these are my tips for how to study for a technical interview

